\label{sec-BK-transformation}
Following the terminology in  \cite{Bigins-EJP}, 
 we say that  the random weight $W$  is in the  {\it  boundary case} or more precisely  in the {\it Biggins-Kyprianou's boundary case},  if it has a {\it Biggins-Kyprianou transform} as follows:
\begin{align}\label{bdary-reg}
W= \frac{e^{-\beta \xi}}{\mathbb{E}[e^{-\beta\xi}]},
\end{align}
where $\beta>0$ is a positive number and $\xi$ is a random variable  taking values in $(-\infty, +\infty]$ such that (we use the convention $(+\infty)\cdot e^{-(+\infty)} = 0$)
\begin{align}\label{xi-cond}
\mathbb{E}[\xi e^{-\xi}] = 0, \quad \mathbb{E}[e^{-\xi}] = \frac{1}{b}
\end{align}
and 
\begin{align}\label{xi-cond-bis}
\mathbb{P}(\xi\in (-\infty, 0))> 0. 
\end{align}

Assume that  $\xi$ satisfies the condition \eqref{xi-cond}. Then $\mathbb{P}(\xi\in (-\infty, 0)) = 0$ if and only if  
\[
\xi = \left\{
\begin{array}{cl}
 0 & \text{with probability $b^{-1}$}
\\
 +\infty &  \text{with probability $1-b^{-1}$}
\end{array}
\right..
\]

\label{ex-bad-boy}
For any $t \in (0,1)$,  consider the random variable 
\[
W_t = \left\{
\begin{array}{cl}
t^{-1}& \text{with probability $t$}
\\
0 & \text{with probability $1- t$}
\end{array}
\right. .
\]
One can check directly that all these random variables are not in the  boundary case. 
However, there is a unique $t = b^{-1}$, such that the random variable $W_{1/b}$ can be written as  $W_{1/b}= e^{-\beta\xi}/\mathbb{E}[e^{-\beta\xi}]$ (with non-unique $\beta>0$), where
$
\xi = 0 \cdot \mathds{1}(W_{1/b}>0) + (+\infty)  \cdot \mathds{1}(W_{1/b}=0).
$
The above $\xi$ satisfies the condition  \eqref{xi-cond}, but violates the condition  \eqref{xi-cond-bis}. Hence by definition, $W_{1/b}$ is not in the boundary case.

